  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    Forty seconds — about thirty-five once five minutes are set aside for the review.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The reading text: ten questions answered from a closed book in about four minutes.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    Flag and return. Leaving it blank gives away a quarter of a mark, and re-reading spends the paper on one question.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    A quarter of a mark — the guess that was not made. Across sixty questions that is up to fifteen marks.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    EXCEPT and NOT — set in the same size as everything else and skipped by the hurrying eye.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    Blanks first: they are the only certain loss. Then negatives, then mis-reads, then transfer.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    Questions first: they tell you what the passage is being read for.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The paragraph decides the register; a word chosen before the sense is clear is a guess.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    One counter-example kills it, and the passage usually supplies one.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The echo looks like evidence and is decoration — the paper plants it deliberately.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    Tone lives in the word choice: the same act can be *firm* or *stubborn* depending on the adjective.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    Coverage is the test. A detail, however true, answers a different question.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    Substitution is the test: if the sentence still works, the reference is right.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    Meaning in context. The four options here would be *inflexible*, *flexible*, *cheerful* and *reluctant*, and only one fits the sentence.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    It has a subject and a verb but cannot stand alone — the definition of a subordinate clause.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    With *neither … nor*, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, *teachers*, which is plural.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *News* is singular despite its ending. The plural-looking form is the trap.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *Accused of* is the fixed collocation, and the other prepositions are the ones a Nigerian speaker might reach for.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The second clause is a consequence of the first, and *therefore* marks consequence.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The earlier of two past events takes the past perfect.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The second conditional takes *were* for all persons — the subjunctive that survives in this formula.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The letter receives the action, so the passive is required, and *yesterday* fixes the past simple.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *Adamant* means refusing to change one's mind; *flexible* is its opposite and *reluctant* is a different idea.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *Candid* means frank; the antonym is *evasive*. The other three are synonyms, which is why they look right.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The idiom is about evasion. Its opposite is to come to the point.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The image is lightning from a clear sky: something sudden and unexpected.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *Seat* and *see* are both /i:/. *Sit* is the short /ɪ/, and *set* and *said* are both /e/.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *Think* is voiceless /θ/; *this*, *then* and *they* carry the voiced /ð/.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *Be-GIN*. The other three are stressed on the first syllable, which is the pattern for most two-syllable nouns and adjectives.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *Find* rhymes: the rhyme runs from the stressed vowel, so *mint* fails on the final consonant.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The stress falls on the word that answers the false claim — *lives* corrects the suggestion that he merely visits.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *k* before *n* is silent: knife, knee, know, knock.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *Comf-ta-ble*: three beats, because the *or* is swallowed. The spelling suggests four.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    Chief Mrs Solape Bayo, the MD's mother, who supplies the proverb about the snake in the roof.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    Chapter 12, *…Dawn*, answers *Dusk* and completes the frame.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The blank needs a noun object of *reached*; the other three are a verb and two adverbs or adjectives.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The adjective follows *was* and takes *to* + infinitive; *reluctantly* is the adverb and the other two are the noun and a non-word.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The flat reporting of an absurd sequence is the criticism: the facts do the work and the adjectives are withheld.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    The oral forms: ten mostly single-word decisions while the mind is fresh. Comprehension and cloze need the passage read.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.47pt)[
    *Those* is one of the voiced grammatical words; *think*, *three* and *health* are all voiceless /θ/.
  ]

]

